// The `memory` sub-library provides the reference-counted handle motor used by
// the runtime. It has no callable API: it is internal C++ support that the
// array/string/object codegen will re-express its heap-backed types on.
//
// Under the hood it generates `core_memory.h`, which implements
// `core_mem::Ref<T>`: a shared-mutable handle to a refcounted heap box with
// correct RAII (copy acquire, move without acquire, `operator=` release-old +
// acquire-new, dtor release + free-if-0). Allocation lives in the same block
// as the payload, so a single `free()` releases both, and no handle operation
// except construction/clone allocates (an OOM yields a null handle rather than
// a throw, matching the no-exceptions embedded runtime).
//
// Migration status onto this single motor:
//   - `Array<T>`: DONE (core_array.h includes core_memory.h and re-expresses
//     Array as Ref<Storage<T>>).
//   - `String` (copy-on-write): DONE (core_string.h includes core_memory.h and
//     re-expresses String as Ref<StrStorage>; the old core_array::Buffer was
//     retired).
//   - object fields: pending.
